# PR#9: real, and the bound that was built for it in this same PR is out again, because
# both attempts at one were worse than the hole. A pending entry carries no honest age:
# XPENDING's idle is the time since the last delivery and every XCLAIM resets it, so an
# entry going round never ages; the stream id is the moment the command was enqueued, not
# the moment it first ran, so a command that waited a day unread and was only just picked
# up looks identical to one that has been going round for a day, and retiring on it drops
# a command that never ran. Knowing when an entry first became pending means writing that
# down per delivery, which is a key per command on the hot path and a key class the
# contract does not have. What is in place instead couples the two lifetimes from the
# other side: Recall pushes the record's expiry out, so a record outlives any entry
# anybody is still asking about. What is left is a fleet down longer than the TTL with a
# command still pending, which is a day-long outage; for a send the caller's message_id
# is what covers the repeat, and the commands where it would bite are the ones the roadmap
# fences on the lease.
